From 11d3330da1021d2320b023f5335b1438a42f2bf1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noah Evans <noah.evans@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 10:03:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/16] hw/usb: model the SMSC LAN9514 ethernet controller

The Raspberry Pi's onboard network adapter, which qemu's raspi machines
have no model for.  A guest driver for it can only be written blind:
qemu offers usb-net, which is CDC ECM, and nothing that answers the
vendor requests and framing an smsc95xx-style driver uses.

This models the ethernet function -- not the hub in front of it on a
Pi, since a guest reaches the device through whatever topology the
machine already provides.  Registers are read and written with vendor
requests 0xa1 and 0xa0 on endpoint 0, four bytes little-endian with
the register offset in wIndex.  Transmit frames carry the two command
words; received frames carry the status word, the FCS, and four byte
alignment padding, and are packed into bursts when MEF and the burst
cap allow it.  The internal PHY answers at address 1 through
MII_ADDR/MII_DATA, and reports a link that is always up.

No EEPROM is fitted, which is the case on a Pi: the MAC comes from the
VideoCore firmware rather than from the chip, so an E2P_CMD completes
immediately with the timeout bit set and a driver is expected to fall
back to another source.

The register layout, reset values and framing come from the
LAN9512/LAN9514 datasheet and Linux's smsc95xx driver, not from any
particular guest driver.

Protocol violations are reported rather than tolerated -- a transmit
whose command words disagree with the length actually transferred, a
transmit with the transmitter disabled, a register access that is not
four bytes, a write to a read-only register, an MII access to an
absent PHY.  The first of those immediately caught a real bug when the
model was first run against a guest.

